{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law for Fractions.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law for Fractions.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cancellation law for fractions cancellation law for fractions","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law for Fractions.md","fileName":"Cancellation Law for Fractions.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cancellation Law for Fractions","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Fractions and Decimal Forms]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Fractions and Decimal Forms","Fraction in Lowest Terms","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law for Fractions.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law for Fractions.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cancellation law for fractions cancellation law for fractions","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law for Fractions.md","fileName":"Cancellation Law for Fractions.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cancellation Law for Fractions","heading":"Cancellation Law for Fractions","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The cancellation law for fractions states that for nonzero natural numbers (n) and (k),\n\n$$\\frac{mk}{nk}=\\frac{m}{n}.$$\n\nThe factor (k) scales both the number of selected parts and the number of parts in a unit, so the represented proportion remains unchanged. The law permits a common factor to be removed from the numerator and denominator when finding a [[Fraction in Lowest Terms]]. It cancels factors, not separate terms joined by addition or subtraction.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Fractions and Decimal Forms","Fraction in Lowest Terms","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellati